        <div id="why-slower" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Why 120V Dryers Are Slower, and How Much
          </h2>

          <p>
            Drying clothes is mostly a matter of evaporating water, and evaporating water takes a fixed amount of energy. A full-size electric dryer gets that energy from a heating element that commonly draws around 5,000 watts on a 30-amp, 240-volt circuit. A standard 120-volt, 15-amp outlet can deliver 1,800 watts at most, and the code expects continuous loads to stay at or below 80 percent of that, about 1,440 watts. Every 120V clothes dryer has to work inside that ceiling, which is why compact portables are rated around 1,400 watts.
          </p>

          <p>
            The arithmetic is unforgiving for resistance-heated portables. With roughly a quarter to a third of the heat of a full-size machine, the same load takes far longer, which is why owners of 120-volt portables commonly report cycles of one to two hours or more for a full drum of cotton. That is not a defect. It is the outlet. No setting or brand will make a 1,400-watt heater behave like a 5,000-watt one.
          </p>

          <p>
            Heat pump dryers get around the ceiling by recycling heat rather than throwing it away. A conventional dryer heats air, passes it through the clothes once and blows it out the vent, carrying most of the energy with it. A heat pump dryer runs the air in a closed loop, using a refrigerant circuit to pull moisture out as water and put the heat back into the air. Because the energy is reused, a heat pump machine can do useful drying on a 120-volt circuit, and models like the Miele T1 and the GE and LG combos are designed around exactly that.
          </p>

          <p>
            They are still not as fast as a 240-volt vented dryer, because heat pumps dry at lower temperatures. But they are gentler on fabric, they do not need a vent, and their total energy per load is far lower. For a full explanation of how heat pump drying works and the 240-volt models worth considering, read our <a href="/best-heat-pump-dryer/">best heat pump dryer</a> guide.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Power at a Glance</p>
