        <div id="types" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. The Three Types of 120V Clothes Dryer
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Compact vented portables</h3>

          <p>
            These are the $250 to $500 machines most people picture when they search for portable dryers: the Panda, BLACK+DECKER and Magic Chef on our list, plus dozens of lookalikes. They are small tumble dryers with a resistance heater, a stainless drum of 1.5 to 3.5 cubic feet and an exhaust port. They plug into any outlet, weigh 35 to 60 pounds and can sit on a counter, a stand or a wall bracket. They are cheap and simple, but slow, and they must be vented somewhere.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Ventless heat pump and condenser dryers</h3>

          <p>
            Standalone ventless dryers condense moisture out of the air and send it to a drain or a tank, so nothing is exhausted into the room. Condenser models use a resistance heater and are slower. Heat pump models recycle heat and are far more efficient. The catch is voltage. Most compact ventless dryers sold in the US, including Bosch's compact condenser and heat pump models, need 208 to 240 volts. Miele's US T1 heat pump dryers are a notable exception at 120 volts, which is why the TXD160WP is on this list.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">All-in-one washer-dryer combos</h3>

          <p>
            Combos wash and dry in the same drum. The newest generation, led by the GE Profile UltraFast Combo and LG WashCombo, pairs a full-width washer with heat pump drying and runs on a standard 120-volt circuit with no vent. Older and smaller combos, such as GE's 24-inch model, use condenser drying, which works but takes longer. The defining benefit is that nobody has to move wet clothes, so a load can go from dirty to dry while you are at work or asleep.
          </p>

          <p>
            As a rule of thumb, a combo makes sense when you have a water supply and drain but no vent or 240-volt outlet. A standalone 120-volt heat pump dryer makes sense when you already have a washer. A vented portable makes sense when the budget is tight, the laundry volume is small and you can route an exhaust hose outside.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="drying-times" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Drying Times and Capacity, Honestly
          </h2>

          <p>
            Manufacturers rarely publish real-world drying times for 120-volt machines, and when they do the conditions are favorable. Here is how to think about it. Drying time depends on how much water is in the load, which depends on fabric and washer spin speed, divided by how much heat the dryer can deliver. A front-load washer spinning at 1,200 rpm or more leaves far less water behind than a top-loader or a hand-wrung load, and that alone can cut drying time substantially.
          </p>

          <p>
            Load size matters more on a 120-volt dryer than on a full-size one. Air needs room to circulate through the clothes, and a packed drum dries slowly and wrinkles badly. With portables, filling the drum about two-thirds full is usually faster overall than one stuffed load, even though it means more loads. With combos, the drying capacity is typically smaller than the washing capacity, so many owners wash a full load and dry it in two batches, or wash smaller loads in the first place.
          </p>

