        <div id="venting" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Venting, Humidity and Lint
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every load of washed clothes holds a surprising amount of water. A spun load of cotton can easily carry several pounds of it, and a dryer's job is to get that water out of the clothes and somewhere else. A vented dryer puts it into the air as warm vapor and pushes it outside. A ventless dryer condenses it back to liquid and sends it down a drain or into a tank. The one thing you do not want is that water ending up in your room.
          </p>

          <p>
            That is exactly what happens when a vented portable runs with no exhaust hose, or with the hose aimed at a corner. Humidity climbs, windows fog, and in a small bathroom or closet you set up the conditions for mold. Fine lint escapes the filter and coats surfaces. Some manufacturers sell portables with a lint bag or a so-called indoor vent kit, which catches lint but does nothing for moisture. If you must run a vented portable indoors without an exterior vent, pair it with a properly sized unit from our <a href="/best-dehumidifier-for-home/">best home dehumidifiers</a> guide.
          </p>

          <p>
            The better answer is a real exhaust path. A window vent kit with a sealed panel and a short, smooth hose works well in rentals and comes out when you leave. Keep the hose as short and straight as possible, use rigid or semi-rigid metal duct where you can rather than thin plastic foil, and clean the lint screen every load. Long, kinked or crushed hoses slow drying and let lint build up, and lint buildup is the leading cause of dryer fires.
          </p>

          <p>
            Ventless heat pump and condenser machines remove most of this worry. They still give off some warmth, so give them the clearances the manual asks for, and they need either a drain connection or a water tank you empty. Clean the lint filter every cycle and the secondary filter or condenser as the manual directs, because a clogged heat pump filter is the most common reason these dryers start running long. If moisture already lingers in your laundry room, see our guide to <a href="/how-to-get-rid-of-mold-in-basement/">getting rid of mold in a basement</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="circuits" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Outlets, Circuits and Breakers
          </h2>

          <p>
            Plugs into any outlet is true for 120-volt dryers in the narrow sense that the plug fits. It is not true that any outlet can run one well. A 1,400-watt portable draws close to 12 amps, which is most of a 15-amp circuit. Share that circuit with a space heater, a microwave, a hair dryer or a window air conditioner and the breaker will trip mid-cycle. Before you buy, find out what else is on the outlet you plan to use; our guide on <a href="/how-to-find-which-breaker-controls-an-outlet/">finding which breaker controls an outlet</a> walks through it.
          </p>

          <p>
            The heat pump combos and the Miele go further and ask for a dedicated circuit. GE specifies an individual, properly grounded branch circuit protected by a 15-amp breaker or time-delay fuse for its UltraFast Combo. That does not require 240 volts or new heavy wiring, but if your laundry spot shares a circuit with the kitchen or bathroom, a dedicated 20-amp or 15-amp circuit is a modest electrician job worth doing.
          </p>

          <p>
            Do not run any dryer on an extension cord or a plug strip, and do not use a GFCI-protected outlet that trips frequently as an excuse to plug into a non-GFCI outlet across the room. Laundry areas generally require GFCI protection under current codes, and modern dryers are designed to run on it. Persistent tripping is a sign of a fault, not of an over-sensitive outlet.
          </p>

          <p>
            If your home does have a 240-volt dryer outlet that does not match your dryer's plug, you do not need a 120-volt machine at all. Our guide to the <a href="/best-dryer-cord-adapter/">best dryer cords and adapters</a> explains how to fix a 3-prong and 4-prong mismatch the right way. And never try to power a 240-volt dryer from a 120-volt outlet through an adapter; it will not heat, and some of the adapters sold online for it are unsafe.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="apartments" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Apartment Dryers and Lease Rules
          </h2>

          <p>
            Before buying an apartment dryer, read your lease. Many leases and condo rules restrict laundry appliances, usually for three reasons: water damage from washers, fire risk from vented dryers with bad ductwork, and electrical load. A small vented portable with a hose out the window can run afoul of all three in the eyes of a building manager, while a ventless dryer for an apartment with no exhaust and no water connection is often easier to get approved.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are adding a washer as well, put a leak pan under it and use braided stainless supply hoses rather than rubber. Water damage to the unit below is the reason most buildings say no, and a pan plus good hoses answers the most common objection. For combos, the drain hose needs a standpipe or a secure connection to a sink drain, not a hose draped over the edge of a tub.
          </p>

          <p>
            Portable dryers are popular in RVs, tiny homes, garages and ADUs for the same reasons they suit apartments. In an RV, check your shore power and inverter capacity, because 1,400 watts on a 30-amp RV service leaves limited room for an air conditioner or water heater. If you are running from a generator or a power station, a dryer is one of the hungriest loads you can add, and a portable power station is a poor match for a resistance-heated dryer.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, think about noise and vibration. A dryer on a counter or wall bracket transmits vibration into the building, and neighbors will hear an unbalanced load at 11 p.m. Rubber pads under freestanding units and solid stud mounting for wall-hung ones go a long way, and combos with direct-drive motors are noticeably quieter than belt-driven portables.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="energy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. What a 120V Dryer Costs to Run
          </h2>

          <p>
            Running cost is easy to estimate from the rated wattage. A 1,400-watt portable running for 90 minutes uses about 2.1 kilowatt-hours. At a US average residential rate of roughly 17 cents per kilowatt-hour, that is about 36 cents a load. The same load in a full-size 240-volt dryer finishes faster but at higher power, and typically lands in a similar range or higher, so a portable is not more expensive to run per load, only slower.
          </p>

          <p>
            Heat pump machines are where the savings are real. GE rates its UltraFast Combo's heat pump drying as roughly 50 percent more energy efficient than ENERGY STAR standard electric dryers, and standalone heat pump dryers like the Miele use a fraction of the energy of a resistance dryer per load. For a household drying five or more loads a week, that adds up to meaningful savings over the life of the machine, though it rarely pays back the price premium over a cheap portable on electricity alone.
          </p>

          <p>
            Condenser combos sit in between. The resistance heater uses about as much energy as a portable, and some older designs also use water to cool the condenser, which adds to the bill in a way that does not show up on the electricity meter. That is part of why the GE 24-inch combo is not ENERGY STAR certified, and why we steer people with the space toward heat pump combos instead.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are curious how other always-on appliances compare, our breakdown of <a href="/how-much-electricity-does-a-dehumidifier-use/">how much electricity a dehumidifier uses</a> uses the same arithmetic, and it is a useful reference if you end up pairing a vented portable with a dehumidifier in a small space.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="skip" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Who Should Skip a 120V Dryer
          </h2>

          <p>
            Families doing more than about eight loads a week will struggle with any 120-volt portable, and may find even a combo limiting because it handles one load at a time. If you have a 240-volt outlet and a vent, a conventional full-size dryer remains the fastest and cheapest way to dry a lot of laundry, and if you have a 240-volt outlet but no vent, a full-size heat pump dryer is the upgrade to look at.
          </p>

          <p>
            Skip the cheapest no-name portables. The category is full of near-identical units sold under rotating brand names, with thin documentation, uncertain safety listings and no real parts support. A dryer is a heating appliance that runs unattended, so we would pay a little more for a brand with US support and a listing mark from a recognized testing lab.
          </p>

          <p>
            Skip vented portables if you cannot vent them. Running one into a closed room trades a laundry problem for a humidity and lint problem, and in a small apartment that can turn into a mold problem. If venting is impossible, save for a ventless heat pump dryer or combo, or use a drying rack and a dehumidifier, which is slower but uses less energy than any dryer.
          </p>

          <p>
            And skip the idea that a 120-volt dryer will feel like the one you grew up with. The right expectation is different, not worse: smaller loads more often, longer cycles you do not have to watch, and in the case of heat pump machines, clothes that come out cooler, softer and less shrunk. Set that expectation and a good 120V clothes dryer is a genuine quality-of-life upgrade over hauling laundry down the street.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running a vented portable with no exhaust</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Without a hose to the outside, a vented portable dumps warm wet air and lint into the room. Windows fog, walls get damp, and small rooms can grow mold. Use a window vent kit or an existing exterior vent, or buy a ventless heat pump model if venting is impossible.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sharing the circuit with other heavy loads</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 1,400-watt dryer uses most of a 15-amp circuit. Add a space heater, microwave or hair dryer and the breaker trips mid-cycle. Check what else is on the outlet first, and give heat pump combos the dedicated circuit their manuals require rather than squeezing them onto a shared one.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Overloading the drum</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A packed drum on a low-power dryer barely moves air and can take hours. Filling a portable about two-thirds full dries faster overall, and many combo owners dry a full wash in two batches. Spinning clothes at the highest washer speed before drying helps more than any dryer setting.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming every compact dryer is 120V</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Many 24-inch European-style dryers sold in the US, including Bosch compact condenser and heat pump models, need 208 to 240 volts. Check the voltage and plug type on the spec sheet before buying. Miele's US T1 heat pump dryers and the combos on our list are confirmed 120-volt.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring lint and filter cleaning</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Lint buildup is the leading cause of dryer fires, and portables with long or kinked hoses collect it fast. Clean the lint screen every load, keep exhaust hoses short and straight, and clean heat pump filters and condensers on schedule. A clogged filter is the most common reason ventless dryers start running long.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <PlanTheJob />

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a clothes dryer run on 120 volts?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. Compact vented portables, a few standalone heat pump dryers such as Miele's US T1 models, and the newer all-in-one combos from GE and LG all run on standard 120-volt outlets. They are slower than 240-volt dryers because a 15-amp outlet limits power to roughly 1,440 watts continuous. Heat pump models make up most of that gap by recycling heat.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does a 120V dryer take to dry a load?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends heavily on type and load size. Vented portables commonly take one to two hours or more for a full drum of cotton. Heat pump combos are faster per pound and run unattended, with GE advertising wash-and-dry cycles as short as about 90 minutes. Smaller loads and a high washer spin speed cut time the most.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do portable dryers need to be vented?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Vented portables do. They exhaust warm, moist, lint-filled air and should be connected to a window vent kit or an exterior vent. Running one into a closed room causes humidity and lint problems. Ventless heat pump and condenser dryers do not need a vent, because they condense the moisture into water and send it to a drain or tank.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best ventless dryer for an apartment?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                If you need both washing and drying, the GE Profile UltraFast Combo or LG WashCombo is the best ventless option on a standard 120-volt circuit. If you already have a washer, the Miele T1 TXD160WP is the strongest standalone 120-volt heat pump dryer. For very tight spaces, GE's 24-inch condenser combo fits where others will not.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I plug a portable dryer into any outlet?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The plug will fit any standard outlet, but the circuit matters. A 1,400-watt dryer uses most of a 15-amp circuit, so avoid outlets shared with heaters, microwaves or air conditioners. Never use an extension cord or power strip. Heat pump combos and the Miele specify a dedicated circuit, which is a modest job for an electrician.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are 120V washer dryer combos worth it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For homes with no vent and no 240-volt outlet, the new heat pump combos are worth it. They handle near full-size loads, run without anyone moving wet clothes and use far less energy than resistance dryers. They are expensive and handle one load at a time, so large households with a vent and 240-volt outlet are better served by a separate pair.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a 120 volt dryer cheaper to run?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Per load, a vented 120-volt portable costs roughly the same as a full-size dryer, about 30 to 40 cents at average US rates, because it runs longer at lower power. Heat pump models are genuinely cheaper, using roughly half the energy of a standard electric dryer or less. Condenser combos fall in between and may also use water.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

